 :findings
 [{:id :shx-plan/validation-operation-envelope
   :priority :P2
   :context :己
   :confidence 0.98
   :location {:path "docs/specs/restricted-bash-v1.md" :lines [139 154]}
   :title "Give validation its own explicit closed operation envelope"
   :evidence "The fixed entries must accept only their matching operation (139-143), but the only concrete envelope has :operation :render (151-152). The next sentence says Validate adds exactly :content and :descriptor, without changing that discriminator. Taken literally it sends :render to the validation-only entry; changing it to :validate is currently an undocumented wire change."
   :impact "Independent BB/NBB clients and the validation helper can implement different interpretations of an otherwise exact, closed protocol. The positive validator call then either fails its matching-operation check or accepts the renderer discriminator. This is a planning ambiguity, not a claim that any validator code already exists."
   :minimal-exact-correction "Replace the envelope paragraph with two explicit closed forms: RenderEnvelope = {:protocol :shx/safe-bash-v1 :request-id ID :operation :render :request Request}; ValidateEnvelope = {:protocol :shx/safe-bash-v1 :request-id ID :operation :validate :request Request :content String :descriptor Descriptor}. State that each entry rejects the other operation and all unknown/missing keys, and retain helper-configured trusted pins outside both wire forms. The existing correlated Response shape can stay unchanged."
   :tests-needed ["B01/B11: each actual fixed entry accepts its own exact envelope on both BB and NBB transports."
                  "B11: send :render to validation and :validate to rendering, including otherwise well-shaped opposite-operation fields; require sanitized protocol rejection/nonzero exit, never success."
                  "Reject absent/unknown operation and request-supplied trusted-pins or executable/classpath fields without invoking any generated content."]
   :settlement "Owner edits only the planning contract, records a new exact head/hash, and obtains independent confirmation of this correction before unrestricted owner RED/GREEN admission."}]
 :accepted-planning-choices
 [{:area :closed-owner-language
   :basis "Spec 16-74, 78-115 and B01-B09 define finite closed literal/ref requests and restricted IR, Unicode/metadata/runtime-value rejection, bounded traversal, exact independent positive parsing and full ordered expected-binding comparison. This does not promote generic IR validity to safety."}
  {:area :canonical-quote-grammar
   :basis "Spec 78-107 pins splice hex 27 22 27 22 27, empty/edge/repeated quote goldens, final LF and no headers/alternative shell syntax. This matches S1A 249-258 while deliberately leaving existing sq backslash-splice and dq expansion semantics unchanged. No renderer import, re-render-only proof, shell parser, bash -n or native evaluation is admitted."}
  {:area :reference-shadowing
   :basis "Spec 67-74 makes preceding-export shadow rejection a request-validity rule and allows self-reference. This is an acceptable conservative owner subset, not a request to redefine generic shx or read environment values. S1B must propagate rejection for the entire affected request/batch, never reorder, drop or replace bindings to obtain success."}
  {:area :owner-projection-bounds
   :basis "Spec 44-64, 155-158 and B08 state owner-smaller admission bounds: 1-128 bindings, names up to 128 characters, 0-1024 available names, 4096-byte literals/paths, 65536-byte artifacts and bounded frames/depth/elements. Limits apply together, including quote expansion. A consumer must visibly hold/reject an oversized projection rather than truncate it or claim the wider S1A schema was narrowed."}
  {:area :independent-identity-and-coverage
   :basis "Spec 109-135 requires exact descriptor metadata, UTF-8 byte count/SHA, independently trusted validator pins, separate renderer pins and transitive semantic bundle coverage. Shared schema/domain dependency changes invalidate applicable pins. Runtime/dependency-lock identities and actual callable entries are additional handoff obligations, not supplied by a Git SHA or producer descriptor. Single-target validation explicitly leaves whole-batch target/descriptor/content bijection to S1B."}
  {:area :owner-layers-and-qualified-runtime
   :basis "Design 45-76 respects Malli-schema-only law, pure domain/parser and shape, with JVM infra. BB/NBB pure fixture parity is distinct from external Malli validation. Both clients must later call the actual fixed, separately pinned JVM renderer and validator process/classpath; fake helpers or assumed Malli-in-BB availability do not qualify this boundary."}
  {:area :spawn-and-effect-boundary
   :basis "Spec 137-179 and B11-B12 prohibit request-selected code/classpaths, shell launch, Clojure CLI dependency/startup resolution, inherited HOME/XDG/CLASSPATH/JVM injection options, personal hooks and secret reads. The 5000-ms budget is spawn-relative, followed by 250-ms TERM grace and 750-ms cleanup budget; it is not permission to wait for JVM readiness before starting the clock."
   :qualification "Actual implementation proof must cover stdin/draining, cold pinned JVM startup/classpath, EOF/exit/stderr, overflow, timeout/cancellation/late-output races and descendants. Terminal failure cannot be reversed by late success; cleanup uncertainty blocks reuse. These are future B11 obligations, not measured timing or cleanup guarantees established by planning prose."}
  {:area :compatibility-and-qualification-gates
   :basis "Design 30-41 and B13 retain generic IR, envm's filesystem-filtered PATH versus runtime IR guards, sq and dq, existing tests and all seven blocking analyzers. Pure changes require mutation survivor/no-coverage inspection, not a score-only assertion or weakened thresholds."}]
